research/audit/wave12-dispatch/audit-refuter-thm-c-k-complete-in-the-sup-metric.prompt.md-891-2.1 Separation (M1): $d_\infty(f,g)$ bounds $D(f,g)$ above, so $d_\infty(f,g) \ge |f(s_0) - g(s_0)| \ge 0$; if $d_\infty(f,g) = 0$ then $|f(s) - g(s)| \le 0$ and $|f(s) - g(s)| \ge 0$ for every $s$, hence $f(s) = g(s)$ for every $s$ and $f = g$; conversely if $f = g$ then $D(f,g) = \{0\}$, whose least upper bound is $0$. [step 1.1, L2, L3, L6]
research/audit/wave12-dispatch/audit-refuter-thm-c-k-complete-in-the-sup-metric.prompt.md-892-
research/audit/wave12-dispatch/audit-refuter-thm-c-k-complete-in-the-sup-metric.prompt.md-893-2.2 For every $s \in S$: $|f(s) - h(s)| = |(f(s) - g(s)) + (g(s) - h(s))| \le |f(s) - g(s)| + |g(s) - h(s)| \le d_\infty(f,g) + d_\infty(g,h)$, the last inequality because each supremum bounds its own set above. [step 1.1, L2, L4, L6]
research/audit/wave12-dispatch/audit-refuter-thm-c-k-complete-in-the-sup-metric.prompt.md-894-
research/audit/wave12-dispatch/audit-refuter-thm-c-k-complete-in-the-sup-metric.prompt.md-895-3.1 Triangle inequality (M3): step 2.2 says the real number $d_\infty(f,g) + d_\infty(g,h)$ is an upper bound of $D(f,h)$, and $d_\infty(f,h)$ is the least upper bound of that set, so $d_\infty(f,h) \le d_\infty(f,g) + d_\infty(g,h)$. [step 2.2, L2]
research/audit/wave12-dispatch/audit-refuter-thm-c-k-complete-in-the-sup-metric.prompt.md-896-
research/audit/wave12-dispatch/audit-refuter-thm-c-k-complete-in-the-sup-metric.prompt.md-897-4.1 The function $d_\infty$ therefore satisfies (M1) by step 2.1, (M2) by step 1.2 and (M3) by step 3.1, so it is a metric on $\mathcal{B}(S)$. [step 1.2, step 2.1, step 3.1] ∎
research/audit/wave12-dispatch/audit-refuter-thm-c-k-complete-in-the-sup-metric.prompt.md-898-
research/audit/wave12-dispatch/audit-refuter-thm-c-k-complete-in-the-sup-metric.prompt.md-899-## Remarks
research/audit/wave12-dispatch/audit-refuter-thm-c-k-complete-in-the-sup-metric.prompt.md-900-
research/audit/wave12-dispatch/audit-refuter-thm-c-k-complete-in-the-sup-metric.prompt.md-901-- **Why the bounded functions and not all functions.** For unbounded $f$ the set $D(f,g)$ need not be bounded above and $\sup D(f,g)$ then does not exist, so $d_\infty$ would not be a function into $\mathbb{R}$ at all. Texts that write $d_\infty(f,g) = +\infty$ in that case are working in the extended real line, which is introduced on a later page. The suprema taken here are real numbers, and the extended real line is not used for them ([[rem-sup-conventions]], [[rem-metric-axiom-conventions]]).
research/audit/wave12-dispatch/audit-refuter-thm-c-k-complete-in-the-sup-metric.prompt.md-902-- **The supremum need not be attained**, so $d_\infty(f,g)$ is genuinely a supremum and not a maximum; the companion page carries a witness.
research/audit/wave12-dispatch/audit-refuter-thm-c-k-complete-in-the-sup-metric.prompt.md:903:- **The name "uniform metric" points at later material.** The quantified definition of uniform convergence of functions appears in [[def-pointwise-uniform-and-uniformly-cauchy-convergence]]. The later [[rem-uniform-convergence-agrees-with-the-later-uniform-topology]] records its agreement with convergence in $d_\infty$; this lemma proves only that $d_\infty$ is a metric on the stated bounded-function space.
