research/audit/wave12-dispatch/certifier-a6-cex-dini-needs-monotonicity-r2.log-11172-research/audit/wave12-dispatch/audit-beta-a4r2.log-8018-research/audit/wave5-topology-function-spaces.provenance.jsonl-19-{"id": "def-equicontinuity", "statement": "literature-derived", "proof": "not-applicable", "evidence": "exact-source", "urls": ["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Equicontinuity", "https://faculty.etsu.edu/gardnerr/5357/notes/Munkres-45.pdf"], "rationale": "All three notions are sourced verbatim in the item's own metric epsilon-delta form. Wikipedia, Equicontinuity: 'The family F is equicontinuous at a point x_0 in X if for every epsilon > 0, there exists a delta > 0 such that d(f(x_0), f(x)) < epsilon for all f in F and all x such that d(x_0, x) < delta'; and 'The family F is uniformly equicontinuous if for every epsilon > 0, there exists a delta > 0 such that d(f(x_1), f(x_2)) < epsilon for all f in F and all x_1, x_2 in X such that d(x_1,x_2) < delta.' Munkres Sec. 45 gives pointwise boundedness verbatim: 'a subset F of C(X,Y) is pointwise bounded under d if for each a in X, the subset F_a = {f(a) | f in F} of Y is bounded under d.' No hypothesis, quantifier or direction changed. Definition, no proof component.", "alpha_concurred": false, "at": "2026-08-05"}
research/audit/wave12-dispatch/certifier-a6-cex-dini-needs-monotonicity-r2.log-11173-research/audit/wave12-dispatch/audit-beta-a4r2.log:8019:research/audit/wave5-topology-function-spaces.provenance.jsonl:20:{"id": "thm-dini", "statement": "ai-altered", "proof": "ai-generated", "evidence": "semantic-source", "urls": ["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dini%27s_theorem", "https://faculty.etsu.edu/gardnerr/5357/notes/Munkres-45.pdf"], "rationale": "Sourced statement: X a compact TOPOLOGICAL space, (f_n) a monotonically increasing sequence of continuous real-valued functions converging pointwise to a continuous f : X -> R; then the convergence is uniform, and the monotonically decreasing form holds too. The item is that statement with X specialised to a compact METRIC space, the monotonicity written out pointwise as f_k(x) <= f_{k+1}(x), and the nonincreasing form recorded as a consequence of applying the theorem to the negatives rather than proved separately. Added hypothesis and reformulation only; conclusion and direction unchanged. Local proof, kept choice-free by taking the finite subcover as a list of indices.", "alpha_concurred": false, "at": "2026-08-05"}
research/audit/wave12-dispatch/certifier-a6-cex-dini-needs-monotonicity-r2.log-11174-research/audit/wave12-dispatch/audit-beta-a4r2.log-8020-research/audit/wave5-topology-function-spaces.provenance.jsonl-21-{"id": "rem-function-space-conventions", "statement": "ai-generated", "proof": "not-applicable", "evidence": "none", "urls": [], "rationale": "Positive determination of novelty: this is a page-conventions remark about THIS LIBRARY's standing hypotheses, notation, reading order and scope. It has no counterpart in the mathematical literature and could not have one, so it is genuinely locally authored rather than merely unsourced. No load-bearing mathematical claim is made and no item depends on it (verified: zero reverse-deps corpus-wide), so the ai-generated statement label creates no dependency-eligibility problem. Follows the established retro-tag precedent for conventions remarks in this corpus (rem-topology-conventions, rem-counting-conventions-and-scope, rem-compactness-choice-ledger-metric, rem-constructions-this-page-stops-short-of, rem-sieve-pigeonhole-and-double-counting-conventions, rem-complete-metrizability-is-the-topological-shadow), all tagged ai-generated with no generation block. Truth-risk check performed: SEE FINDING F1, which reports two false clauses in section 1 of this remark.", "alpha_concurred": false, "at": "2026-08-05"}
research/audit/wave12-dispatch/certifier-a6-cex-dini-needs-monotonicity-r2.log-11175-research/audit/wave12-dispatch/audit-beta-a4r2.log-8021---
research/audit/wave12-dispatch/certifier-a6-cex-dini-needs-monotonicity-r2.log-11176-research/audit/wave12-dispatch/audit-beta-a4r2.log-8022-research/audit/wave5-topology-function-spaces.provenance.jsonl-32-{"id": "ex-the-exponential-law-worked-on-the-line", "statement": "ai-altered", "proof": "ai-generated", "evidence": "semantic-source", "urls": ["https://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/compact-open+topology", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Compact-open_topology", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Exponential_object"], "rationale": "A worked instance of the sourced exponential law on X = Z = Y = R, with the multiplication map as the chosen f. Multiplication on R x R is continuous, its transpose is the family of multiplication-by-z maps, each Lipschitz with constant |z|, and local compactness of R is Munkres Sec. 29 Example 1. Every ingredient is standard; the composition into one traced example, and the direct estimate |x||z-z_0| <= iota(m)|z-z_0| on [-m,m] replacing the tube lemma, are local. Hence ai-altered. Local verification.", "alpha_concurred": false, "at": "2026-08-05"}
research/audit/wave12-dispatch/certifier-a6-cex-dini-needs-monotonicity-r2.log-11177-research/audit/wave12-dispatch/audit-beta-a4r2.log:8023:research/audit/wave5-topology-function-spaces.provenance.jsonl:33:{"id": "ex-dini-on-the-unit-interval", "statement": "ai-altered", "proof": "ai-generated", "evidence": "semantic-source", "urls": ["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dini%27s_theorem", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Uniform_limit_theorem"], "rationale": "A worked instance of the sourced Dini theorem on [0,1] together with its standard sharpness discussion. The clipped identities s_k(t) = min{t, 1 - a_k} form a nondecreasing sequence of continuous functions converging pointwise to the continuous identity, which is a routine instance rather than a novel construction, and the failure clause reuses this page's ramps to show that continuity of the limit cannot be dropped, which is the standard remark accompanying Dini's theorem. Specialisation of a sourced theorem plus a standard sharpness witness, hence ai-altered. Local verification; note that the positive half is also proved directly by the bound |s_k(t) - t| <= a_k, independent of Dini.", "alpha_concurred": false, "at": "2026-08-05"}
